This extension adds Welcome to Elderfield modding support to Vortex Mod Manager.

[b]✅ Supported Versions:[/b]
[list]
[*]Steam[/*]
[*]Steam Demo[/*]
[*]Other versions may need to select the installation location manually.[/*]
[/list]
[b]🛠️ Mod Installation Notes:[/b]
[list]
[*]Js folder mods with a "js" folder install to the game folder.[/*]
[*]Js file mods with a ".js" file install to the "js\plugins" folder.[/*]
[*]Root Folder mods with an "Elderfield" folder install to the game folder.[/*]
[*]JSON mods with a ".json" file install to the "data" folder.[/*]
[*]Any other mod not described above installs to the game folder.[/*]
[*]Archives with a FOMOD installer (a "fomod" folder with ModuleConfig.xml) are handed to Vortex's built-in FOMOD installer instead.[/*]
[/list]
[b]📋 Usage Notes:[/b]
[list]
[*]You can open several useful files/folders/URLs using the buttons within the folder icon on the Mods toolbar.[/*]
[*]Installs .js plugin mods to the "js/plugins" folder. Includes Load Order support that writes a basic entry for each mod to the plugins.js file. You can also enter custom description and parameters in the Load Order page using the pencil icon.  [b]Read each mod page's install instructions carefully to ensure the correct data is entered in plugins.js. [/b][/*]
[/list]

[b]📝 Notes for Mod Authors:[/b]
[spoiler]
Packaging rules for Welcome to Elderfield mods, so Vortex installs them to the right place.

Vortex decides what a mod is by looking at the files and folders inside the archive. It tries each installer in order and the first one that matches wins, so archive layout is what determines where your mod ends up.

========================================================================

js folder:

Recognised when the archive contains a "js" folder.

  Installs to: the game folder

========================================================================

js file:

Recognised when the archive contains a ".js" file.

  Installs to: "js\plugins"

========================================================================

Root / Game Folder Mods:

For mods laid out the same way the files appear inside the game folder. Vortex copies the matched folder and everything under it straight into the game.

[code]
MyRootMod.zip
└── Elderfield\
    └── ... files in their real relative locations
[/code]

  Requirements:
    - Recognised by a folder named "Elderfield", "audio", "css", "data", "effects", "fonts", "icon", "img", "lib", "locales" or "swiftshader" in the archive.

  Installs to: the game folder

  Common mistakes:
    - Zipping the folder that CONTAINS the game folders, instead of the game folders themselves, adds an extra level and misplaces every file.

========================================================================

JSON Mod:

Recognised when the archive contains a ".json" file.

  Installs to: "data"

========================================================================

Fallback Installer:

The catch-all. Any archive that matched none of the installers above lands here and is copied across unchanged.

[b][color=#ffff00]NOTE: Landing in the fallback installer is a signal your archive layout needs fixing.[/color][/b]

  Requirements:
    - Reaching this installer usually means the archive was not laid out in a way Vortex recognised.
    - Vortex shows the user a notification when a mod installs through the fallback.

  Installs to: the game folder

  Common mistakes:
    - If your mod lands here unintentionally, re-check the layouts above - users will see a fallback warning and may report the mod as broken.

========================================================================

Rules That Apply To Every Mod Type:

    - Archives that contain a FOMOD installer (a "fomod" folder with "ModuleConfig.xml") are handed to Vortex's built-in FOMOD installer instead, and none of the rules above apply.
    - Folder and file name matching is case-insensitive.
    - Extra wrapper folders around a recognised folder are generally fine; the installer searches at any depth.
[/spoiler]

